WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.228 --> 00:00:05.770
CALUM BARNES: Good
morning, everyone.

00:00:05.770 --> 00:00:09.570
Thanks for joining us
on day three of I/O

00:00:09.570 --> 00:00:13.020
to talk about Thread
and Google Cloud.

00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:14.130
My name's Calum Barnes.

00:00:14.130 --> 00:00:17.130
I'm a product manager on
Google Cloud IoT Core.

00:00:17.130 --> 00:00:19.380
JONATHAN HUI: And I'm Jonathan
Hui, principal software

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:22.710
engineer at Google Nest and VP
of technology for the Thread

00:00:22.710 --> 00:00:24.910
group.

00:00:24.910 --> 00:00:28.060
CALUM BARNES: So we're super
excited about this session

00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:29.950
today and to tell
you a little bit

00:00:29.950 --> 00:00:33.670
about things that are happening
in Thread and IoT Core.

00:00:33.670 --> 00:00:36.190
Before we get started,
can we just see--

00:00:36.190 --> 00:00:37.480
maybe show of hands--

00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:43.220
how many of you here have
heard about Thread before?

00:00:43.220 --> 00:00:43.788
OK.

00:00:43.788 --> 00:00:44.330
Most of them.

00:00:44.330 --> 00:00:46.570
That's pretty good, Jonathan.

00:00:46.570 --> 00:00:47.830
And what about IoT Core?

00:00:47.830 --> 00:00:51.410
Or used Google Cloud
in any way before?

00:00:51.410 --> 00:00:52.240
Also pretty good.

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:53.060
OK.

00:00:53.060 --> 00:00:55.520
So if you haven't heard
about them, don't worry.

00:00:55.520 --> 00:00:57.680
We're going to give
a little brief intro

00:00:57.680 --> 00:00:59.120
when we get started here.

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:02.300
If you have, I think we have a
really, really exciting session

00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:05.060
today, because we are
going to be demoing,

00:01:05.060 --> 00:01:09.470
for the first time, MQTT and
TCP over a Thread network

00:01:09.470 --> 00:01:10.670
all the way up to the cloud.

00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:13.670
And we're also going to be
showing co-op communication

00:01:13.670 --> 00:01:14.510
to IoT Core.

00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:19.670
But before we get
started, I want

00:01:19.670 --> 00:01:23.600
to talk a little bit about what
our requirements for the device

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:26.570
that we're going to
build and demo today are,

00:01:26.570 --> 00:01:29.810
and how they differ from,
maybe, some of the current IoT

00:01:29.810 --> 00:01:32.630
devices, whether they be smart
home or commercial buildings

00:01:32.630 --> 00:01:33.933
that are on the market.

00:01:33.933 --> 00:01:35.850
So for the devices we're
going to build today,

00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:37.880
we want them to be
very, very low-power.

00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:40.550
So this means not just
having long battery life,

00:01:40.550 --> 00:01:43.610
but also being able to work
on very, very small batteries

00:01:43.610 --> 00:01:45.200
for long amounts of time.

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:48.080
Next, we want no proprietary
hubs or gateways.

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:51.230
We don't want to have
proprietary application layers

00:01:51.230 --> 00:01:54.110
or require users to
install new hardware

00:01:54.110 --> 00:01:57.200
for every single
device that they buy.

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:00.020
And finally, we want IP
all the way to the edge.

00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:03.230
So we want our devices to work
with the existing internet

00:02:03.230 --> 00:02:05.630
standards and protocols
that we know are secure

00:02:05.630 --> 00:02:07.870
and are comfortable
working with.

00:02:07.870 --> 00:02:11.020
But before we get into how
we're going to do that,

00:02:11.020 --> 00:02:13.885
I want to talk about the state
of wireless products today.

00:02:13.885 --> 00:02:15.510
And I think this is
true whether you're

00:02:15.510 --> 00:02:19.740
talking about smart home, smart
buildings, industrial wireless

00:02:19.740 --> 00:02:20.280
products--

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:21.930
I think it really
runs the gamut.

00:02:21.930 --> 00:02:26.220
So today we have a lot of
devices that are using Wi-Fi,

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:27.570
using 802.11.

00:02:27.570 --> 00:02:30.760
And this works great for
many classes of devices.

00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:33.180
If you have a camera,
smartphone, or laptop,

00:02:33.180 --> 00:02:35.160
something where you can
recharge the battery,

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:39.240
you need to use a lot of
bandwidth, Wi-Fi is great.

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:42.350
But for devices that need
to be very low-power,

00:02:42.350 --> 00:02:43.970
Wi-Fi isn't as good.

00:02:43.970 --> 00:02:47.240
You need to either be able to
recharge the device frequently,

00:02:47.240 --> 00:02:51.530
like your laptop or phone, or
you need to do a lot of tricks

00:02:51.530 --> 00:02:56.860
to end up making it be
very low-power over time.

00:02:56.860 --> 00:03:00.010
The other issue
with Wi-Fi is when

00:03:00.010 --> 00:03:01.550
you start having lots of nodes.

00:03:01.550 --> 00:03:04.870
So especially for your average
consumer's home wireless

00:03:04.870 --> 00:03:05.590
setup--

00:03:05.590 --> 00:03:08.050
if you want to connect every
single light bulb or light

00:03:08.050 --> 00:03:10.120
switch in your
house, you're going

00:03:10.120 --> 00:03:13.870
to have an issue doing that
with your traditional router

00:03:13.870 --> 00:03:17.130
and still have the rest
of your devices work.

00:03:17.130 --> 00:03:18.600
So we know that
even though there's

00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:20.920
a lot of good Wi-Fi
infrastructure in place,

00:03:20.920 --> 00:03:22.470
it's probably not
the technology we

00:03:22.470 --> 00:03:25.590
want to use to build our
next-generation wireless

00:03:25.590 --> 00:03:28.160
and IoT products.

00:03:28.160 --> 00:03:30.747
So I know what a lot of
you might be thinking.

00:03:30.747 --> 00:03:33.080
Well, there are good solutions
out there for this today.

00:03:33.080 --> 00:03:33.890
We have Bluetooth.

00:03:33.890 --> 00:03:35.810
We have ZigBee, we have Z-Wave--

00:03:35.810 --> 00:03:38.450
a number of wireless
technologies

00:03:38.450 --> 00:03:41.750
that will help us build lower
bandwidth but lower power

00:03:41.750 --> 00:03:45.830
consumption and also scale
to large numbers of nodes

00:03:45.830 --> 00:03:48.180
in small spaces.

00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:52.010
However, while these do
address a number of the issues

00:03:52.010 --> 00:03:54.440
that we're looking for, they
have one critical fallback,

00:03:54.440 --> 00:03:57.760
and that's that they need
lots of these IoT gateways.

00:03:57.760 --> 00:04:01.700
So since they depend on their
own radio technologies--

00:04:01.700 --> 00:04:05.030
usually proprietary application
layers and tightly linked

00:04:05.030 --> 00:04:07.340
application back ends--

00:04:07.340 --> 00:04:08.930
you can end up in
situations where,

00:04:08.930 --> 00:04:11.780
if you have multiple products
from multiple vendors--

00:04:11.780 --> 00:04:15.080
or even, in some cases, multiple
products from the same vendor--

00:04:15.080 --> 00:04:19.790
where you end up with a whole
host of these devices connected

00:04:19.790 --> 00:04:22.430
to your router at
home, or it can

00:04:22.430 --> 00:04:26.820
be even worse in industrial
or commercial IoT settings.

00:04:26.820 --> 00:04:30.250
And while this does work
from a technical perspective,

00:04:30.250 --> 00:04:32.980
I think it hurts user
adoption in a couple of ways.

00:04:32.980 --> 00:04:34.365
One is it drives up cost.

00:04:34.365 --> 00:04:39.330
If you need to sell a gateway
with the first light bulb

00:04:39.330 --> 00:04:42.540
that your customer buys, or the
first whatever your product may

00:04:42.540 --> 00:04:45.690
be, you're probably
more than doubling

00:04:45.690 --> 00:04:49.140
the cost for that very
first entry point.

00:04:49.140 --> 00:04:53.010
Next is it creates confusing
user setup experiences.

00:04:53.010 --> 00:04:54.900
Nobody wants to
have a lot of these

00:04:54.900 --> 00:04:58.410
or go through having to learn
the differences between setting

00:04:58.410 --> 00:05:00.510
up a Wi-Fi device versus
a Bluetooth device

00:05:00.510 --> 00:05:02.770
versus a Zigbee device.

00:05:02.770 --> 00:05:07.230
And finally-- and I think
what the largest problem is--

00:05:07.230 --> 00:05:08.880
is it encourages silos.

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:10.890
So the manufacturers
of these gateways

00:05:10.890 --> 00:05:13.540
want every product to
work with their ecosystem,

00:05:13.540 --> 00:05:16.560
and so we end up with
silos of functionality as

00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:18.570
opposed to real network
interoperability

00:05:18.570 --> 00:05:20.863
like we want to build.

00:05:20.863 --> 00:05:22.530
So I think these are
some real problems.

00:05:22.530 --> 00:05:24.750
But luckily, we're
pretty close to having

00:05:24.750 --> 00:05:26.470
a solution to help with it.

00:05:31.403 --> 00:05:33.570
JONATHAN HUI: So we were
facing these exact problems

00:05:33.570 --> 00:05:36.330
that Calum was talking
about at Nest, where

00:05:36.330 --> 00:05:39.720
we wanted to build products
for the connected home

00:05:39.720 --> 00:05:43.770
where we were keeping users
not only comfortable, but also

00:05:43.770 --> 00:05:46.150
safe and secure.

00:05:46.150 --> 00:05:48.390
We wanted to build solutions
where our devices could

00:05:48.390 --> 00:05:52.530
communicate securely with
each other to cloud services

00:05:52.530 --> 00:05:55.710
and to users via
their mobile devices.

00:05:55.710 --> 00:05:58.770
We also wanted to have those
devices to be very low-power,

00:05:58.770 --> 00:06:01.410
because we wanted those
devices to last for years

00:06:01.410 --> 00:06:04.730
on very small batteries.

00:06:04.730 --> 00:06:08.400
But as we evaluated existing
communication technologies,

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:10.470
we could not find a
single technology that

00:06:10.470 --> 00:06:13.770
satisfied our requirements.

00:06:13.770 --> 00:06:17.900
So in 2014, Nest, along with
a number of other companies,

00:06:17.900 --> 00:06:19.910
founded The Thread Group.

00:06:19.910 --> 00:06:22.550
The Thread Group is a
non-profit organization

00:06:22.550 --> 00:06:24.230
with more than 100
member companies

00:06:24.230 --> 00:06:26.130
from around the world.

00:06:26.130 --> 00:06:28.850
And The Thread Group is
responsible for defining

00:06:28.850 --> 00:06:32.510
and maintaining the Thread
protocol specification.

00:06:32.510 --> 00:06:34.610
it serves as a market
education group

00:06:34.610 --> 00:06:37.940
for promoting the use of
Thread in connected products.

00:06:37.940 --> 00:06:41.120
And it ensures a
great user experience

00:06:41.120 --> 00:06:43.280
through a rigorous
and meaningful product

00:06:43.280 --> 00:06:46.320
certification program.

00:06:46.320 --> 00:06:48.690
So in summary, the
thread protocol

00:06:48.690 --> 00:06:53.670
is an open, IP-based, low-power,
secure mesh networking

00:06:53.670 --> 00:06:57.400
technology for
connecting IoT devices.

00:06:57.400 --> 00:06:59.820
It supports the
same IP technology

00:06:59.820 --> 00:07:02.430
that drives every
internet-connected device,

00:07:02.430 --> 00:07:05.950
but is designed to support
very low-power applications,

00:07:05.950 --> 00:07:10.750
including devices that operate
on coin cell batteries.

00:07:10.750 --> 00:07:15.210
Now, for the radio,
Thread uses IEEE 802.15.4.

00:07:15.210 --> 00:07:19.410
It's the same radio that's been
used in ZigBee for 15 years.

00:07:19.410 --> 00:07:21.990
Using an existing
widely-deployed radio

00:07:21.990 --> 00:07:25.500
allows Thread products to
achieve fast time to market,

00:07:25.500 --> 00:07:28.410
as well as leverage
existing economies of scale

00:07:28.410 --> 00:07:31.800
to achieve low-cost solutions.

00:07:31.800 --> 00:07:33.570
Now, at the network
layer, Thread

00:07:33.570 --> 00:07:36.660
uses existing IETF
standards for supporting

00:07:36.660 --> 00:07:42.480
the internet protocol version
6, or IPv6, over 802.15.4,

00:07:42.480 --> 00:07:47.200
radios, a technology
called 6LoWPAN.

00:07:47.200 --> 00:07:49.100
Now, Thread support for
the internet protocol

00:07:49.100 --> 00:07:52.880
is a key differentiator, and
most existing technologies

00:07:52.880 --> 00:07:54.590
do not support IP.

00:07:54.590 --> 00:07:56.910
So why is IP so powerful?

00:07:56.910 --> 00:08:00.590
Well, first it means that we
can build solutions that utilize

00:08:00.590 --> 00:08:04.550
a number of link technologies,
including Wi-Fi, cellular,

00:08:04.550 --> 00:08:08.600
and Thread, and have all of
those devices communicating

00:08:08.600 --> 00:08:12.530
with a common single
application layer end-to-end

00:08:12.530 --> 00:08:15.980
without needing to build and
maintain application protocol

00:08:15.980 --> 00:08:18.195
gateways.

00:08:18.195 --> 00:08:19.570
And at the same
time, it means we

00:08:19.570 --> 00:08:21.370
can have a single
network that can

00:08:21.370 --> 00:08:25.300
support multiple
applications simultaneously.

00:08:25.300 --> 00:08:28.300
IP makes it easy to
deploy new applications,

00:08:28.300 --> 00:08:31.570
and it was a key reason for
the success of the internet.

00:08:31.570 --> 00:08:36.600
Thread extends that benefit
to low-power devices.

00:08:36.600 --> 00:08:38.880
Specifically for
this session, it

00:08:38.880 --> 00:08:41.370
means that we can build
battery-powered devices

00:08:41.370 --> 00:08:45.300
that communicate securely
end-to-end with Google Cloud

00:08:45.300 --> 00:08:49.392
IoT Core, without any
application layer in between.

00:08:49.392 --> 00:08:50.850
CALUM BARNES: OK,
so now we've seen

00:08:50.850 --> 00:08:54.810
how we are going to use
Thread as the radio technology

00:08:54.810 --> 00:08:56.140
to build these products.

00:08:56.140 --> 00:08:57.870
And we also know
that Thread is going

00:08:57.870 --> 00:09:00.360
to give us an underlying
IP layer link, which

00:09:00.360 --> 00:09:03.570
means we can use just
about any cloud back end

00:09:03.570 --> 00:09:06.960
that we want to connect
these products and leverage

00:09:06.960 --> 00:09:08.970
data from them or
interact with them.

00:09:12.180 --> 00:09:13.790
But let's talk
about first what we

00:09:13.790 --> 00:09:17.150
need to think about when
we choose a cloud product

00:09:17.150 --> 00:09:19.430
or choose a cloud to use.

00:09:19.430 --> 00:09:21.420
There are a lot of
options out there,

00:09:21.420 --> 00:09:24.140
and I think a lot of them
will probably satisfy

00:09:24.140 --> 00:09:25.820
what we're going to do today.

00:09:25.820 --> 00:09:28.520
But there are two big things
that you want to think about.

00:09:28.520 --> 00:09:31.700
First is security, and
the second is scale.

00:09:31.700 --> 00:09:35.000
So security is always
important in IoT.

00:09:35.000 --> 00:09:38.630
It's a very often
written-about topic.

00:09:38.630 --> 00:09:41.960
I'm sure I don't need to
tell people at IO about all

00:09:41.960 --> 00:09:44.720
of the high-profile
leaks that have happened

00:09:44.720 --> 00:09:47.970
or botnet attacks
that have happened.

00:09:47.970 --> 00:09:54.870
But what is going to drive
more security in IoT,

00:09:54.870 --> 00:09:57.180
I think, starts with
identity and having

00:09:57.180 --> 00:09:59.812
a strong underlying
identity for our products.

00:09:59.812 --> 00:10:01.770
So we want to make sure
that whatever we choose

00:10:01.770 --> 00:10:05.310
is going to allow us to
put a cryptographically

00:10:05.310 --> 00:10:09.360
strong and unique
identity on the boards

00:10:09.360 --> 00:10:11.870
that we're going to
use in the demo today.

00:10:11.870 --> 00:10:13.040
And next is scale.

00:10:13.040 --> 00:10:15.560
I think there's a
few things to think

00:10:15.560 --> 00:10:18.770
about when you are
thinking about scale

00:10:18.770 --> 00:10:20.210
in the context of IoT.

00:10:20.210 --> 00:10:23.863
One is we are thinking about
large numbers of devices.

00:10:23.863 --> 00:10:25.280
So over time, we
think there might

00:10:25.280 --> 00:10:28.250
be millions or tens
of millions of devices

00:10:28.250 --> 00:10:30.278
that we might sell
into market and need

00:10:30.278 --> 00:10:31.320
to connect to this cloud.

00:10:31.320 --> 00:10:33.380
So we need a
solution that we know

00:10:33.380 --> 00:10:36.320
can scale up to very
large numbers of devices.

00:10:36.320 --> 00:10:39.990
But we should also realize
that, at the very beginning,

00:10:39.990 --> 00:10:42.680
your IoT deployment or
solution might be just

00:10:42.680 --> 00:10:46.540
like ours is onstage, where you
have just one or two devices.

00:10:46.540 --> 00:10:48.670
And for this reason,
you want whatever

00:10:48.670 --> 00:10:52.150
you choose to scale from
an economic perspective

00:10:52.150 --> 00:10:56.560
when you still have just
a few devices in market.

00:10:56.560 --> 00:11:00.370
So to do this today, we're going
to use Google Cloud IoT Core.

00:11:00.370 --> 00:11:03.520
IoT Core is Google
Cloud's managed service

00:11:03.520 --> 00:11:07.970
for connecting IoT devices
to the Google Cloud network.

00:11:07.970 --> 00:11:10.820
It provides two important things
that we're going to show today.

00:11:10.820 --> 00:11:12.460
So first is device identity.

00:11:12.460 --> 00:11:15.220
It provides a mechanism
for distributing

00:11:15.220 --> 00:11:17.030
key-based identities to devices.

00:11:17.030 --> 00:11:20.830
So every device will end up
with its own unique private key,

00:11:20.830 --> 00:11:27.093
as well as the corresponding
public key trusted in IoT Core.

00:11:27.093 --> 00:11:29.260
These keys can be used in
a couple of different ways

00:11:29.260 --> 00:11:31.010
to connect and
authenticate to the system,

00:11:31.010 --> 00:11:34.260
and Jonathan is going to
talk more about that later.

00:11:34.260 --> 00:11:36.980
The other thing it offers is
a protocol bridge and support

00:11:36.980 --> 00:11:42.860
for common IoT protocols
like MQTT and HTTP.

00:11:42.860 --> 00:11:45.210
But this protocol bridge
is completely serverless

00:11:45.210 --> 00:11:46.980
and will scale with
our application.

00:11:46.980 --> 00:11:49.920
So it's easy to connect
just a few devices to it,

00:11:49.920 --> 00:11:52.710
and we'll only have to
pay for the bandwidth used

00:11:52.710 --> 00:11:54.340
by those couple of devices.

00:11:54.340 --> 00:11:57.240
But it will also easily
scale up to hundreds

00:11:57.240 --> 00:12:00.300
of thousands or millions
of devices deployed all

00:12:00.300 --> 00:12:01.770
over the globe,
without us having

00:12:01.770 --> 00:12:05.065
to change any code or deal
with managing the back end.

00:12:08.180 --> 00:12:10.670
But connecting the
devices to the cloud

00:12:10.670 --> 00:12:11.870
isn't the whole story.

00:12:11.870 --> 00:12:15.440
We need to actually communicate
back to the devices or leverage

00:12:15.440 --> 00:12:18.050
the data that they're sending
to us if we want to build

00:12:18.050 --> 00:12:20.060
an actually useful application.

00:12:20.060 --> 00:12:24.370
And luckily, Google Cloud
can help with this, too.

00:12:24.370 --> 00:12:28.120
So we're going to, today, try
to leverage all serverless tools

00:12:28.120 --> 00:12:32.230
that will scale elastically with
your application the same way

00:12:32.230 --> 00:12:34.470
that I just talked
about IoT Core doing.

00:12:34.470 --> 00:12:36.220
So the first thing
that we're going to use

00:12:36.220 --> 00:12:37.540
is Google Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:12:37.540 --> 00:12:42.400
So all data sent from
IoT devices into IoT Core

00:12:42.400 --> 00:12:46.360
end up as a message on
a Cloud Pub/Sub topic.

00:12:46.360 --> 00:12:48.070
Pub/Sub, if you're
not familiar with it,

00:12:48.070 --> 00:12:52.330
is a scalable message bus to
build event-driven serverless

00:12:52.330 --> 00:12:54.910
or microservice applications.

00:12:54.910 --> 00:12:57.730
So you'll never have to
worry about scaling for peaks

00:12:57.730 --> 00:13:01.702
or dealing with losing data.

00:13:01.702 --> 00:13:03.910
The next thing we're going
to use is Cloud Functions.

00:13:03.910 --> 00:13:06.610
So a Cloud Function is
a small snippet of code

00:13:06.610 --> 00:13:08.920
that can be written in a
few different languages that

00:13:08.920 --> 00:13:12.940
are supported right
now, and it allows you

00:13:12.940 --> 00:13:14.630
to do event-driven development.

00:13:14.630 --> 00:13:17.620
So this function can fire
every time an event occurs

00:13:17.620 --> 00:13:19.582
or at a regular interval.

00:13:19.582 --> 00:13:21.040
In this case, we're
going to use it

00:13:21.040 --> 00:13:23.740
to process each message
in each event that

00:13:23.740 --> 00:13:26.570
comes into our Pub/Sub stream.

00:13:26.570 --> 00:13:29.250
This will make it easy to,
again, scale our application,

00:13:29.250 --> 00:13:31.530
but also build it.

00:13:31.530 --> 00:13:33.750
And finally, we're going
to use Cloud Firestore.

00:13:33.750 --> 00:13:37.460
Firestore is a flexible NoSQL
database hosted and managed

00:13:37.460 --> 00:13:38.750
by Google Cloud.

00:13:38.750 --> 00:13:41.780
And you'll see the query system
and real-time update nature

00:13:41.780 --> 00:13:44.420
will make it easy for us
to build this application,

00:13:44.420 --> 00:13:47.330
but also, in the future,
build a mobile application

00:13:47.330 --> 00:13:50.570
or other application
that leverages that data.

00:13:50.570 --> 00:13:53.390
Google Cloud has dozens
of other services

00:13:53.390 --> 00:13:55.580
that can help with
building your application,

00:13:55.580 --> 00:13:57.997
but these are the few that
we're going to use for the demo

00:13:57.997 --> 00:13:58.635
today.

00:13:58.635 --> 00:14:00.010
JONATHAN HUI: All
right, so let's

00:14:00.010 --> 00:14:02.093
get to some of the fun
stuff and actually show you

00:14:02.093 --> 00:14:04.338
how we can build some of it.

00:14:04.338 --> 00:14:07.090
As I mentioned,
Thread support for IP

00:14:07.090 --> 00:14:09.820
makes it possible
for a Thread device

00:14:09.820 --> 00:14:14.260
to communicate securely and
end-to-end with IoT Core.

00:14:14.260 --> 00:14:16.420
In the middle, there is
a Thread border router

00:14:16.420 --> 00:14:18.220
that's responsible
for forwarding

00:14:18.220 --> 00:14:20.440
packets between
the Thread network

00:14:20.440 --> 00:14:22.190
and the public internet.

00:14:22.190 --> 00:14:24.460
And note that the Thread
border router simply forwards

00:14:24.460 --> 00:14:27.070
IP packets at the network
layer, and does not

00:14:27.070 --> 00:14:28.930
need to implement
any application layer

00:14:28.930 --> 00:14:30.790
logic or proxy.

00:14:30.790 --> 00:14:32.410
In fact, the Thread
border router

00:14:32.410 --> 00:14:35.650
has no knowledge
of MQTT or HTTP,

00:14:35.650 --> 00:14:38.590
making it easy to deploy new
applications without having

00:14:38.590 --> 00:14:40.690
to update or maintain
application layer

00:14:40.690 --> 00:14:43.840
proxies in between.

00:14:43.840 --> 00:14:46.270
Now, three years
ago, here at I/O,

00:14:46.270 --> 00:14:49.060
we launched the
OpenThread Project.

00:14:49.060 --> 00:14:52.360
OpenThread is an open-source
implementation of Thread,

00:14:52.360 --> 00:14:54.100
and it is the same
implementation

00:14:54.100 --> 00:14:58.360
that's used in Nest products,
including the Nest Hub Max that

00:14:58.360 --> 00:15:02.290
was announced here at I/O.

00:15:02.290 --> 00:15:04.900
The Open Thread Project
is hosted on GitHub

00:15:04.900 --> 00:15:08.320
and licensed under BSD-3.

00:15:08.320 --> 00:15:10.300
And it's seen
widespread adoption.

00:15:10.300 --> 00:15:14.110
OpenThread has been ported to
more than 10 different 802.15.4

00:15:14.110 --> 00:15:16.840
radios, and you can
find example drivers

00:15:16.840 --> 00:15:20.230
for each of those on GitHub.

00:15:20.230 --> 00:15:25.060
Now, in today's demo, we'll
use the Nordic nRF52840 system

00:15:25.060 --> 00:15:26.080
on chip.

00:15:26.080 --> 00:15:28.302
We actually brought a few
of those here with us today

00:15:28.302 --> 00:15:30.760
for use in our demo, so let's
take a look at them on stage.

00:15:33.850 --> 00:15:36.180
You'll see a couple
dev boards on the right

00:15:36.180 --> 00:15:39.270
and then a USB dongle attached
to a Raspberry Pi on the left.

00:15:42.060 --> 00:15:47.450
The nRF52840 is built around
a 32-bit Arm Cortex-M4

00:15:47.450 --> 00:15:51.120
with 256k of RAM,
1 meg of flash.

00:15:51.120 --> 00:15:53.490
And they're really intended
for low-power, low-cost

00:15:53.490 --> 00:15:54.880
applications.

00:15:54.880 --> 00:15:57.100
Now, these dashboards
are publicly available,

00:15:57.100 --> 00:16:00.030
which means you can go home
and replicate everything

00:16:00.030 --> 00:16:01.900
we've done on stage today.

00:16:01.900 --> 00:16:05.780
All right, so let's
get back to the slides.

00:16:05.780 --> 00:16:09.397
Let's get started with MQTT.

00:16:09.397 --> 00:16:10.980
Now, for those of
you that don't know,

00:16:10.980 --> 00:16:15.410
MQTT stands for Message
Queuing Telemetry Transport

00:16:15.410 --> 00:16:18.260
and is one of the most
popular transport protocols

00:16:18.260 --> 00:16:20.730
used in IoT applications today.

00:16:20.730 --> 00:16:24.110
MQTT implements a
publish/subscribe model

00:16:24.110 --> 00:16:24.980
on top of TCP/IP.

00:16:27.580 --> 00:16:29.700
Now, in this example,
we'll walk through how

00:16:29.700 --> 00:16:33.360
to build an MQTT over
Thread endpoint that

00:16:33.360 --> 00:16:38.550
communicates securely,
end-to-end, with IoT Core.

00:16:38.550 --> 00:16:40.620
We will leverage
third-party software.

00:16:40.620 --> 00:16:44.490
For networking, we'll use
Lightweight IP, or LwIP,

00:16:44.490 --> 00:16:46.920
allowing us to leverage
application frameworks that

00:16:46.920 --> 00:16:51.510
have already been built on
top of LwIP, including MQTT.

00:16:51.510 --> 00:16:55.500
For security, this example
leverages mbedTLS and a JWT C

00:16:55.500 --> 00:16:58.340
library for security.

00:16:58.340 --> 00:17:01.800
And for the RTOS,
we'll use FreeRTOS.

00:17:01.800 --> 00:17:04.810
Now let's walk through
some of the code.

00:17:04.810 --> 00:17:08.170
So here's the main control loop
that runs on the Thread device.

00:17:08.170 --> 00:17:09.220
It's pretty simple.

00:17:09.220 --> 00:17:13.079
It starts by establishing
an MQTT session to IoT Core,

00:17:13.079 --> 00:17:15.810
and then it sits in a while
loop, publishing telemetry

00:17:15.810 --> 00:17:19.359
events every two seconds.

00:17:19.359 --> 00:17:22.930
So let's dive into the
connect method first.

00:17:22.930 --> 00:17:26.569
The first step is to specify
the MQTT connection parameters.

00:17:26.569 --> 00:17:30.250
MQTT uses TSL for
transport layer security,

00:17:30.250 --> 00:17:32.710
so we have to set up the
private key and certificates

00:17:32.710 --> 00:17:35.150
for use for TLS.

00:17:35.150 --> 00:17:37.120
We also need to
specify other MQTT

00:17:37.120 --> 00:17:41.980
parameters, such as the client
ID and keep alive values.

00:17:41.980 --> 00:17:44.190
Now, at the bottom,
the device must also

00:17:44.190 --> 00:17:46.830
prepare a JSON Web Token.

00:17:46.830 --> 00:17:50.616
Let's take a closer look at
what that JSON Web Token is.

00:17:50.616 --> 00:17:52.990
Now, as Calum
mentioned, IoT Core

00:17:52.990 --> 00:17:55.970
provides a device
identity system.

00:17:55.970 --> 00:18:01.290
A JSON Web Token, or JWT,
is a compact URL-safe means

00:18:01.290 --> 00:18:05.530
of representing claims, and is
used for device authentication

00:18:05.530 --> 00:18:07.490
with IoT Core.

00:18:07.490 --> 00:18:10.240
A JWT is composed
of three sections--

00:18:10.240 --> 00:18:12.310
a header, a payload
that contains

00:18:12.310 --> 00:18:14.970
a claim set, and a signature.

00:18:14.970 --> 00:18:17.490
The header and payload
are JSON objects.

00:18:17.490 --> 00:18:19.540
And the header
consists of two fields

00:18:19.540 --> 00:18:21.340
that indicate the
signing algorithm

00:18:21.340 --> 00:18:23.170
and the type of the token.

00:18:23.170 --> 00:18:26.620
The example here uses
RSA, but IoT Core also

00:18:26.620 --> 00:18:29.200
supports elliptic curve.

00:18:29.200 --> 00:18:31.410
Now, the payload contains
a set of claims that is

00:18:31.410 --> 00:18:33.780
signed using asymmetric keys.

00:18:33.780 --> 00:18:36.245
IoT Core requires three claims--

00:18:36.245 --> 00:18:40.050
the Issued At, which is the
timestamp at when the token was

00:18:40.050 --> 00:18:44.040
created, the Expiration,
that is the timestamp when

00:18:44.040 --> 00:18:48.270
the token stops becoming
valid, an Audience

00:18:48.270 --> 00:18:50.760
that contains the
cloud project ID

00:18:50.760 --> 00:18:53.890
where the device is
registered, and then, finally,

00:18:53.890 --> 00:18:56.770
the Signature contains a
cryptographic signature

00:18:56.770 --> 00:18:58.765
of the concatenated
header and payloads.

00:19:01.320 --> 00:19:04.840
So, in code, creating
a job looks like this.

00:19:04.840 --> 00:19:07.740
It leverages the JWT C
library when we first

00:19:07.740 --> 00:19:11.880
create the object, then set the
signing algorithm for the JWT,

00:19:11.880 --> 00:19:14.400
we add the Issued
At, Expiration,

00:19:14.400 --> 00:19:16.560
and Audience claims.

00:19:16.560 --> 00:19:19.320
And finally, we
code jwt_encode_str,

00:19:19.320 --> 00:19:21.600
which generates the
signature and writes it out

00:19:21.600 --> 00:19:24.683
in the proper encoding.

00:19:24.683 --> 00:19:26.850
So, that's how we generate
a client's authentication

00:19:26.850 --> 00:19:29.770
material for IoT Core,
and more generally,

00:19:29.770 --> 00:19:34.110
the MQTT connection parameters.

00:19:34.110 --> 00:19:35.600
The next step is
to perform a DNS

00:19:35.600 --> 00:19:37.910
Lookup for the MQTT endpoint.

00:19:37.910 --> 00:19:41.660
In this case,
mwtt.googleapis.com.

00:19:41.660 --> 00:19:44.550
Now, with the IP address
for the MQTT endpoint,

00:19:44.550 --> 00:19:48.890
we can then initiate
an MQTT connection.

00:19:48.890 --> 00:19:50.600
So, that was connect.

00:19:50.600 --> 00:19:53.300
Pretty simple.

00:19:53.300 --> 00:19:56.240
So, now, let's take a look at
how to publish telemetry events

00:19:56.240 --> 00:19:56.810
using MQTT.

00:19:59.390 --> 00:20:01.190
In this example,
the telemetry events

00:20:01.190 --> 00:20:04.320
are really simple
temperature data.

00:20:04.320 --> 00:20:06.830
The first step is to format
the payload, in this case,

00:20:06.830 --> 00:20:11.510
using JSON, and then we call
MQTT Publish with the payload

00:20:11.510 --> 00:20:15.520
and specify an MQTT topic of
/devices/mqtt-device/events.

00:20:18.410 --> 00:20:22.280
MQTT-device specifies
the device ID.

00:20:22.280 --> 00:20:25.610
And events indicates that
this is a telemetry event.

00:20:25.610 --> 00:20:27.147
That's it.

00:20:27.147 --> 00:20:27.730
Pretty simple.

00:20:30.430 --> 00:20:32.350
So, now, let's
take a look at what

00:20:32.350 --> 00:20:34.240
how this works in a live demo.

00:20:34.240 --> 00:20:37.030
In this demo, we'll be
using a Nordic dev board

00:20:37.030 --> 00:20:39.195
as the MQTT endpoint.

00:20:39.195 --> 00:20:40.570
The Nordic dev
board communicates

00:20:40.570 --> 00:20:45.970
securely end-to-end with
IoT Core's MQTT bridge.

00:20:45.970 --> 00:20:48.210
Now, the thread border router
will use a Raspberry Pi

00:20:48.210 --> 00:20:50.880
with the Nordic USB dongle.

00:20:50.880 --> 00:20:54.180
For the cloud application, we'll
have a simple Cloud Function

00:20:54.180 --> 00:20:56.580
that processes telemetry
events and saves them

00:20:56.580 --> 00:20:58.783
to a Firestore database.

00:20:58.783 --> 00:21:01.200
So, let's take a quick look
at the physical devices again.

00:21:04.490 --> 00:21:08.120
So, the dev board in the
middle will be the one

00:21:08.120 --> 00:21:10.180
that we use for
the MQTT endpoint,

00:21:10.180 --> 00:21:13.900
and that'll communicate
wirelessly via the Raspberry Pi

00:21:13.900 --> 00:21:16.550
end-to-end with IoT Core.

00:21:16.550 --> 00:21:19.540
So now let's switch over
to my laptop and show you

00:21:19.540 --> 00:21:22.003
some live demos.

00:21:22.003 --> 00:21:23.420
So first thing
I'll do is actually

00:21:23.420 --> 00:21:25.910
pull up on the cloud side.

00:21:25.910 --> 00:21:28.170
Just kind of show you to
the Cloud Function here.

00:21:28.170 --> 00:21:32.330
So, the Cloud Function listens
for the topic temperature

00:21:32.330 --> 00:21:34.070
events.

00:21:34.070 --> 00:21:36.680
And each time that
it's triggered,

00:21:36.680 --> 00:21:40.160
it simply decodes
the payload, which

00:21:40.160 --> 00:21:42.530
includes the temperature
data, and puts that

00:21:42.530 --> 00:21:44.820
into a Firestore database.

00:21:44.820 --> 00:21:47.810
It's a very simple
Cloud Function.

00:21:47.810 --> 00:21:49.590
And then, in our
Firestore database

00:21:49.590 --> 00:21:53.260
here, we have a collection
that's just for this demo.

00:21:53.260 --> 00:21:56.720
And then, we have a document
for the MQTT device itself.

00:21:56.720 --> 00:22:02.930
And of course, we have some
data that's set to 0 for now.

00:22:02.930 --> 00:22:05.900
Now, if we go over here,
I have a console open

00:22:05.900 --> 00:22:07.580
to a Raspberry Pi.

00:22:07.580 --> 00:22:11.370
So, as you know, the Raspberry
Pi is the Thread border router.

00:22:11.370 --> 00:22:14.650
If I do an IF config,
you'll see at the bottom,

00:22:14.650 --> 00:22:18.290
the wpan0 interface is
the network interface that

00:22:18.290 --> 00:22:20.143
represents the Thread network.

00:22:20.143 --> 00:22:21.560
As you can see,
the Thread network

00:22:21.560 --> 00:22:24.830
itself appears as a standard
Linux network interface,

00:22:24.830 --> 00:22:28.890
just like Wi-Fi or ethernet.

00:22:28.890 --> 00:22:31.370
What I'm going to do
next is start a TCP dump

00:22:31.370 --> 00:22:35.630
on the wpan0 interface so we can
kind of see some traffic that's

00:22:35.630 --> 00:22:39.010
going over that Thread network.

00:22:39.010 --> 00:22:41.850
Now, what I have also
attached to my laptop

00:22:41.850 --> 00:22:48.550
is the MQTT endpoint, so
I'll open a terminal there.

00:22:48.550 --> 00:22:49.980
And it has a very simple--

00:22:49.980 --> 00:22:51.870
remember, this is
an embedded device,

00:22:51.870 --> 00:22:55.830
so it doesn't even
have a proper terminal,

00:22:55.830 --> 00:22:59.690
but it has a very simple CLI.

00:22:59.690 --> 00:23:02.000
And I do IP Adder to
list these IP addresses.

00:23:02.000 --> 00:23:05.480
Of course, nothing's listed
yet because it's not connected.

00:23:05.480 --> 00:23:11.910
So if I bring the interface up
and start the Thread service

00:23:11.910 --> 00:23:14.110
and wait a moment,
you'll see now

00:23:14.110 --> 00:23:15.880
that it has IP addresses
because it's now

00:23:15.880 --> 00:23:17.650
connected to the
Thread network and has

00:23:17.650 --> 00:23:19.608
obtained the necessary
information to configure

00:23:19.608 --> 00:23:22.700
those IP addresses.

00:23:22.700 --> 00:23:26.900
And finally, I'll
start the MQTT service,

00:23:26.900 --> 00:23:29.130
go back over to the TCP dump.

00:23:29.130 --> 00:23:32.510
The first thing you'll see
is an NTP request-response.

00:23:32.510 --> 00:23:36.780
That's the device itself
synchronizing with global time.

00:23:36.780 --> 00:23:40.870
And then, after
some amount of time,

00:23:40.870 --> 00:23:42.910
you'll start to see TCP traffic.

00:23:42.910 --> 00:23:45.772
That TCP traffic is the
MQTT session itself.

00:23:45.772 --> 00:23:47.230
Of course, we can't
see the payload

00:23:47.230 --> 00:23:50.660
because it's within a
TLS-encrypted session,

00:23:50.660 --> 00:23:54.200
but this is to show you there's
actual TCP traffic happening.

00:23:54.200 --> 00:23:57.910
You'll see messages pop up
about every two seconds.

00:23:57.910 --> 00:24:02.570
And you'll see the actual
data being presented here liv.

00:24:02.570 --> 00:24:05.020
And every time it
receives a new message,

00:24:05.020 --> 00:24:09.600
you'll see that field popping.

00:24:09.600 --> 00:24:10.710
So that's it.

00:24:10.710 --> 00:24:12.980
So that was our first
demo, demonstrating

00:24:12.980 --> 00:24:16.400
MQTT talking end-to-end
securely with IoT Core.

00:24:20.528 --> 00:24:22.320
CALUM BARNES: All right,
awesome, Jonathan.

00:24:22.320 --> 00:24:23.230
[AUDIENCE CLAPPING]

00:24:23.230 --> 00:24:24.270
Yeah.

00:24:24.270 --> 00:24:26.940
I believe that is the
first time, publicly,

00:24:26.940 --> 00:24:30.960
that we've demoed any TCP
protocol and MQTT over a Thread

00:24:30.960 --> 00:24:32.500
network.

00:24:32.500 --> 00:24:35.250
So, that's pretty exciting,
going all the way to IoT Core

00:24:35.250 --> 00:24:39.660
and then up into our very
simple database application.

00:24:39.660 --> 00:24:45.030
So, that's really great,
but MQTT and TCP still

00:24:45.030 --> 00:24:46.688
require a fair amount of power.

00:24:46.688 --> 00:24:48.480
This would maybe be
good for a device which

00:24:48.480 --> 00:24:51.990
has mains power, is
connected all the time,

00:24:51.990 --> 00:24:54.420
but that you still need
low-latency communication back

00:24:54.420 --> 00:24:57.750
down to, like a light
switch or a lightbulb.

00:24:57.750 --> 00:25:00.545
But now, let's think about a
much more constrained device.

00:25:00.545 --> 00:25:01.920
I talked about,
at the beginning,

00:25:01.920 --> 00:25:06.900
wanting to be able to power
a device on just a small coin

00:25:06.900 --> 00:25:08.410
cell battery.

00:25:08.410 --> 00:25:12.030
So, let's talk about
using CoAP with IoT Core.

00:25:12.030 --> 00:25:13.620
So, if you're not
familiar with CoAP,

00:25:13.620 --> 00:25:15.690
it's the Constrained
Application Protocol,

00:25:15.690 --> 00:25:17.940
and it's a specialized
web transfer protocol

00:25:17.940 --> 00:25:20.940
that's made for, basically,
exactly what we want

00:25:20.940 --> 00:25:22.920
to do-- very constrained
devices and using

00:25:22.920 --> 00:25:25.350
very small amounts of power.

00:25:25.350 --> 00:25:28.560
CoAP is designed to
be a lot like HTTP,

00:25:28.560 --> 00:25:32.010
and it implements the
widely used REST model.

00:25:32.010 --> 00:25:36.090
So, servers make resources
available under a URL,

00:25:36.090 --> 00:25:38.220
and clients access
those resources

00:25:38.220 --> 00:25:43.170
using the familiar REST methods,
like get, put, post, delete.

00:25:43.170 --> 00:25:45.530
CoAP was intentionally
designed to resemble

00:25:45.530 --> 00:25:48.310
HTTP to make it
easier for developers

00:25:48.310 --> 00:25:51.830
to build applications with it,
but there are some differences.

00:25:51.830 --> 00:25:54.580
So, at the network layer, you
can see it's exactly the same--

00:25:54.580 --> 00:25:56.645
IPv4 or IPv6.

00:25:56.645 --> 00:25:58.270
And that's exactly
what's going to make

00:25:58.270 --> 00:26:00.940
it easy to use with
our Thread network,

00:26:00.940 --> 00:26:03.640
but the primary difference
is on the transport layer.

00:26:03.640 --> 00:26:08.020
With CoAP, we'll be using
UDP as opposed to TCP.

00:26:08.020 --> 00:26:10.570
And because of that, for
transport layer security,

00:26:10.570 --> 00:26:13.720
we'll also need to use DTLS,
or Datagram Transport Layer

00:26:13.720 --> 00:26:15.940
Security, which is
specifically designed

00:26:15.940 --> 00:26:18.460
to function similar
to TLS and provide

00:26:18.460 --> 00:26:22.750
similar levels of security
for UDP connections.

00:26:22.750 --> 00:26:24.910
And finally, at the
top, we'll use CoAP,

00:26:24.910 --> 00:26:26.650
but from a developer's
standpoint,

00:26:26.650 --> 00:26:32.570
this should look mostly like
access and HTTP interfaces.

00:26:32.570 --> 00:26:37.430
Unfortunately, IoT Core does
not natively support CoAP.

00:26:37.430 --> 00:26:42.030
But because CoAP and
HTTP are so similar,

00:26:42.030 --> 00:26:44.600
it's very easy to
build, implement, or use

00:26:44.600 --> 00:26:46.190
one that's already out there--

00:26:46.190 --> 00:26:49.050
stateless CoAP to HP proxies.

00:26:49.050 --> 00:26:50.960
So, in this case,
what happens is

00:26:50.960 --> 00:26:54.440
we'll use the proxy
URI option in CoAP--

00:26:54.440 --> 00:26:58.820
an option in CoAP is basically
the same as a header in HTTP--

00:26:58.820 --> 00:27:02.630
and we will include information
about the URI of the address

00:27:02.630 --> 00:27:07.700
that we would like the
proxy to access for us.

00:27:07.700 --> 00:27:09.830
And what will happen,
then, is the proxy

00:27:09.830 --> 00:27:12.530
will initiate and
make the HTTP request,

00:27:12.530 --> 00:27:14.390
receive the response
from the server,

00:27:14.390 --> 00:27:18.590
and then return the response
as a CoAP response, which is

00:27:18.590 --> 00:27:20.330
a little different than HTTP.

00:27:20.330 --> 00:27:23.840
But this allows the CoAP
endpoint to use a RESTful HTTP

00:27:23.840 --> 00:27:27.680
service pretty much as if it
was accessing it directly,

00:27:27.680 --> 00:27:33.950
but still getting the benefits
of low-power design and CoAP.

00:27:33.950 --> 00:27:36.780
It's important to note
that the CoAP HTTP proxy--

00:27:36.780 --> 00:27:39.380
Jonathan will talk more about
code availability later--

00:27:39.380 --> 00:27:42.740
but the one that we'll use in
this demo is available for you

00:27:42.740 --> 00:27:44.030
to go download and run.

00:27:44.030 --> 00:27:46.820
It's completely stateless, so
it should scale very easily

00:27:46.820 --> 00:27:51.710
with any application using CoAP
and the IoT Core HTTP bridge.

00:27:54.320 --> 00:27:56.350
So, without further
ado, let's get into it

00:27:56.350 --> 00:27:57.440
and then, see it happen.

00:27:57.440 --> 00:27:59.910
JONATHAN HUL: Yeah.

00:27:59.910 --> 00:28:02.400
Let's see how we can
build a CoAP endpoint that

00:28:02.400 --> 00:28:04.830
communicates with IoT Core.

00:28:04.830 --> 00:28:08.760
Now, as with the MQTT
example, Thread Support for IP

00:28:08.760 --> 00:28:12.510
makes it possible to
communicate securely end to end.

00:28:12.510 --> 00:28:14.813
But compared to
the MQTT example,

00:28:14.813 --> 00:28:16.980
one difference is the
introduction of the CoAP proxy

00:28:16.980 --> 00:28:19.110
that Calum was talking about.

00:28:19.110 --> 00:28:20.790
Now, just to remind
everyone, the proxy

00:28:20.790 --> 00:28:24.840
provides password authentication
passively to IoT Core,

00:28:24.840 --> 00:28:27.890
relaying device credentials
directly to IoT Core.

00:28:27.890 --> 00:28:31.410
And the proxy doesn't do
anything to validate it.

00:28:31.410 --> 00:28:33.210
The incoming collect
payload is converted

00:28:33.210 --> 00:28:38.540
to the required format
for IoT Core HTTP bridge.

00:28:38.540 --> 00:28:41.940
And the responses from
IoT Core's HTTP bridge

00:28:41.940 --> 00:28:44.790
will be returned to the client
with an appropriate CoAP

00:28:44.790 --> 00:28:46.890
response code.

00:28:46.890 --> 00:28:48.930
Now, here's the
software architecture.

00:28:48.930 --> 00:28:51.210
You may note that
this appears much

00:28:51.210 --> 00:28:54.150
simpler than our MQTT example.

00:28:54.150 --> 00:28:56.640
This example runs in bare
minimal environments,

00:28:56.640 --> 00:28:58.830
and does not include an RTOS.

00:28:58.830 --> 00:29:02.100
This example also does not
include a third-party IP stack

00:29:02.100 --> 00:29:04.710
and other application
frameworks.

00:29:04.710 --> 00:29:08.770
The CoAP protocol is actually
used within Thread itself.

00:29:08.770 --> 00:29:11.670
So this example can actually
leverage the same CoAP

00:29:11.670 --> 00:29:15.210
implementation that's
within Open Thread.

00:29:15.210 --> 00:29:17.940
So, in short, what makes this
example really attractive

00:29:17.940 --> 00:29:21.240
is the ability to operate in
very constrained devices that

00:29:21.240 --> 00:29:25.610
have limited memory
and code space.

00:29:25.610 --> 00:29:28.050
Again, the code has two
high-level functions.

00:29:28.050 --> 00:29:30.790
The first is to establish
a secure connection,

00:29:30.790 --> 00:29:33.560
and then followed by
publishing telemetry events

00:29:33.560 --> 00:29:36.120
via that secure connection.

00:29:36.120 --> 00:29:37.760
So, let's take a
look at establishing

00:29:37.760 --> 00:29:39.860
that secure connection first.

00:29:39.860 --> 00:29:43.365
For simplicity, this example
uses a hardcoded IP address,

00:29:43.365 --> 00:29:44.990
but we could have
done that using a DNS

00:29:44.990 --> 00:29:48.020
lookup as in the MQTT example.

00:29:48.020 --> 00:29:50.390
We also need to set up
the security credentials

00:29:50.390 --> 00:29:53.630
for establishing
the secure session.

00:29:53.630 --> 00:29:56.720
Here, we use a pre-assured
key cipher suite.

00:29:56.720 --> 00:29:59.720
Finally, we call OT
CoAP Secure Connect

00:29:59.720 --> 00:30:01.630
to establish the DTLS session.

00:30:04.550 --> 00:30:08.220
So, let's take a look at the
CoAP HTTP proxy implementation

00:30:08.220 --> 00:30:09.660
in this demo.

00:30:09.660 --> 00:30:12.720
In this example, the CoAP proxy
listens on a specific path--

00:30:12.720 --> 00:30:14.700
slash GCP.

00:30:14.700 --> 00:30:18.960
And the proxy URI option
includes the request URI path

00:30:18.960 --> 00:30:22.470
that the CoAP proxy uses
when translating that request

00:30:22.470 --> 00:30:25.040
on behalf of the CoAP endpoint.

00:30:25.040 --> 00:30:28.632
And it includes things like
the project ID, device ID,

00:30:28.632 --> 00:30:29.840
and the publish event action.

00:30:32.410 --> 00:30:34.680
So, with CoAP, we
use the POST method

00:30:34.680 --> 00:30:37.150
to publish telemetry events.

00:30:37.150 --> 00:30:40.050
The first part of generating
the CoAP POST requests

00:30:40.050 --> 00:30:42.630
is to set up the CoAP header.

00:30:42.630 --> 00:30:46.500
This includes specifying
the path, GCP, and the CoAP

00:30:46.500 --> 00:30:49.530
proxy URI option, along
with the publish event

00:30:49.530 --> 00:30:54.430
sub path that indicates that
this is a telemetry event.

00:30:54.430 --> 00:30:58.130
So, let's dive into that proxy
URI option a little more.

00:30:58.130 --> 00:31:00.800
First, we format
the proxy URI path

00:31:00.800 --> 00:31:03.770
that the CoAP proxy uses when
translating this request.

00:31:03.770 --> 00:31:06.320
Again, including things like
the project ID, registry ID,

00:31:06.320 --> 00:31:07.940
device ID.

00:31:07.940 --> 00:31:10.520
And then, we include the JWT,
which includes the claims that

00:31:10.520 --> 00:31:12.620
specify the Issued
At, Expiration,

00:31:12.620 --> 00:31:16.600
and Audience claims that
we talked about earlier.

00:31:16.600 --> 00:31:18.610
We then pass that
proxy URI string

00:31:18.610 --> 00:31:22.020
to JWT Create for encoding
and signature generation.

00:31:22.020 --> 00:31:24.040
And once we have
the JWT, we add that

00:31:24.040 --> 00:31:28.860
to the CoAP header in
the proxy URI option.

00:31:28.860 --> 00:31:32.810
So, that was generating the CoAP
header with a proxy URI option.

00:31:32.810 --> 00:31:37.250
Now let's take a look at
the CoAP payload itself.

00:31:37.250 --> 00:31:38.510
Again, it's pretty simple.

00:31:38.510 --> 00:31:42.890
Here, we format the telemetry
event data in JSON format

00:31:42.890 --> 00:31:45.280
as we did in the MQTT example.

00:31:45.280 --> 00:31:49.310
And then, we append that to the
payload of the CoAP message.

00:31:49.310 --> 00:31:51.280
Pretty simple.

00:31:51.280 --> 00:31:53.710
So now, with a complete
CoAP request message,

00:31:53.710 --> 00:31:55.670
we can send that request
to the CoAP proxy.

00:31:58.320 --> 00:32:00.540
So now, let's take
a look at, again,

00:32:00.540 --> 00:32:01.760
how this looks in action.

00:32:01.760 --> 00:32:03.995
Again, we'll use a Nordic
dev board that operates up

00:32:03.995 --> 00:32:05.930
to this CoAP endpoint.

00:32:05.930 --> 00:32:08.750
And that Nordic dev board
communicates wirelessly, again,

00:32:08.750 --> 00:32:12.620
via the Raspberry Pi, serving
as the Thread border router.

00:32:12.620 --> 00:32:15.140
Difference here is that rather
than communicating directly

00:32:15.140 --> 00:32:19.610
to IoT Core, it is communicating
with the CoAP proxy.

00:32:19.610 --> 00:32:22.790
The CoAP proxy, then,
statelessly translates

00:32:22.790 --> 00:32:25.820
that message into something
that IoT Core can understand,

00:32:25.820 --> 00:32:28.800
which is HTTP.

00:32:28.800 --> 00:32:30.180
Now, on the cloud
side, again, we

00:32:30.180 --> 00:32:33.900
use the same exact Cloud
Function and Firestore database

00:32:33.900 --> 00:32:36.540
as we did with the MQTT demo.

00:32:36.540 --> 00:32:38.280
So, let's go back to my laptop.

00:32:41.380 --> 00:32:43.270
So, here, the same
screen that I left it.

00:32:43.270 --> 00:32:45.280
You can still see
the MQTT device still

00:32:45.280 --> 00:32:48.640
publishing data
every two seconds.

00:32:48.640 --> 00:32:50.300
But I have another
document here,

00:32:50.300 --> 00:32:52.990
which is for the
CoAP device itself.

00:32:52.990 --> 00:32:55.060
And, of course, because
I haven't turned it on,

00:32:55.060 --> 00:32:59.320
the data is still set to zero.

00:32:59.320 --> 00:33:02.190
Now, if I move back to
the console real quick,

00:33:02.190 --> 00:33:04.860
again, TCP traffic flowing
through the Thread border

00:33:04.860 --> 00:33:08.430
router on the Thread interface.

00:33:08.430 --> 00:33:11.160
And on the desk here,
if I pick it up,

00:33:11.160 --> 00:33:12.560
here's the CoAP endpoint.

00:33:12.560 --> 00:33:14.760
It's a Nordic dev board.

00:33:14.760 --> 00:33:18.220
The difference here, rather
than being plugged in, just

00:33:18.220 --> 00:33:20.590
to show how low-power
it is, this dev board,

00:33:20.590 --> 00:33:24.570
actually, is powered
off a coin cell battery.

00:33:24.570 --> 00:33:29.750
Right, so, all I'm going to
do is turn the dev board on.

00:33:29.750 --> 00:33:33.770
You'll see some green
LEDs, hopefully, blinking,

00:33:33.770 --> 00:33:36.050
and what you start to
see here is, again,

00:33:36.050 --> 00:33:39.990
an NTP message for this
device to get global time.

00:33:39.990 --> 00:33:44.030
You start to see some UDP
traffic up in the console.

00:33:44.030 --> 00:33:48.310
That was the DTLS session
itself being established.

00:33:48.310 --> 00:33:50.280
And in this case, it
is event-triggered, so,

00:33:50.280 --> 00:33:54.630
if I push a button here,
you'll see UDP messages.

00:33:54.630 --> 00:33:58.680
And those UDP messages
actually are the CoAP messages

00:33:58.680 --> 00:34:01.350
being transmitted by this
device through the Thread border

00:34:01.350 --> 00:34:03.410
router.

00:34:03.410 --> 00:34:06.800
And if I switch back to the
Firestore database, the CoAP

00:34:06.800 --> 00:34:12.929
device information,
you can see now,

00:34:12.929 --> 00:34:14.880
that counter value
is being updated

00:34:14.880 --> 00:34:17.730
each time I press the button.

00:34:17.730 --> 00:34:19.139
Pretty cool.

00:34:19.139 --> 00:34:21.429
So, that was the CoAP demo.

00:34:21.429 --> 00:34:23.310
CALUM BARNES: Now,
what we've shown

00:34:23.310 --> 00:34:27.570
is two Thread devices talking
through the same border router,

00:34:27.570 --> 00:34:29.730
using standard IP technology.

00:34:29.730 --> 00:34:33.420
In this case, we are
talking to the same service

00:34:33.420 --> 00:34:35.969
and the same ultimate database,
but the important thing

00:34:35.969 --> 00:34:40.078
to note here is we could just as
easily have been talking to two

00:34:40.078 --> 00:34:41.370
completely different back ends.

00:34:41.370 --> 00:34:44.520
These could be two devices
from two different vendors,

00:34:44.520 --> 00:34:46.949
but they are interoperating
at the network level,

00:34:46.949 --> 00:34:49.139
and going through the
same border router

00:34:49.139 --> 00:34:50.699
without having to
worry about or have

00:34:50.699 --> 00:34:53.409
any knowledge of each other.

00:34:53.409 --> 00:34:55.139
So, I think that pretty
much wraps it up.

00:34:55.139 --> 00:34:58.200
Hopefully, this shows
how you, as developers,

00:34:58.200 --> 00:35:01.440
can think about and use Thread.

00:35:01.440 --> 00:35:04.120
And as border routers
become more prevalent,

00:35:04.120 --> 00:35:06.060
you can worry about just
building your devices

00:35:06.060 --> 00:35:08.520
and having them talk to
your back end services,

00:35:08.520 --> 00:35:12.340
wherever they might be hosted,
using standard IP technologies.

00:35:12.340 --> 00:35:16.380
So, if you want to see
the tutorials and code,

00:35:16.380 --> 00:35:20.410
we have it posted for both
the demos at these links.

00:35:20.410 --> 00:35:23.880
And if you have
questions, Jonathan and I

00:35:23.880 --> 00:35:25.620
will be right
outside afterwards.

00:35:25.620 --> 00:35:27.530
So, thanks very much.

00:35:27.530 --> 00:35:29.230
JONATHAN HUL: Thank you.

